           INTERNET CONTEST CHALLENGES BEST AND BRIGHTEST
           TO STRETCH LIMITS OF HIGH-PERFORMANCE COMPUTING


MAYNARD, Mass. -- May 17, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced it is sponsoring a contest for software developers 
designed to stretch the limits of computing as we know it today. The 
contest is co-sponsored by the Pittsburgh Supercomputing Center 
(PSC).

     The contest will be run on Digital's latest high performance 
system, the AlphaServer 8400 5/300 computer with six processors and 
six gigabytes of memory. The system will be accessible to entrants 
over the Internet, and entries will be tested and verified on it at 
the close of the entrance period.

     "Digital has put supercomputer power within reach of a much 
broader spectrum of organizations with its AlphaServer 8400 system," 
said Pauline Nist, vice president of Digital's Server Business Unit 
and contest sponsor. "This contest allows the best and brightest 
software designers to let their imaginations run wild and submit 
their most far reaching proposals as entries."

     There are two categories in the contest, each awarding a prize 
of $8,400 in cash. The categories are: 

     o  A High Performance Fortran (HPF) contest, designed to push 
        the limits of HPF in developing parallel applications.  The 
        scope of applications will be restricted to technical market 
        analysis and any of the earth sciences (climate mapping, 
        signal processing, geophysical, oil and gas exploration).  
        The HPF winner will be judged on:  portability between a 
        workstation farm and the AlphaServer 8400 5/300 symmetric 
        multiprocessor (SMP) machine; scalability on a 4-node 
        workstation farm, with at least a factor of 3 speedup, 
        measured performance, and relevance of the code to 
        production applications. 

     o 	A contest designed to make use of the AlphaServer 8400 5/300 
        technology in a way that advances scientific research.  
        Areas of study could be in (but are not limited to) 
        medicine, earth sciences, chemistry, pharmaceutical, oil and 
        gas, use of science in manufacturing (eg. computational 
        fluid dynamics, molecular modeling, etc.) and signal 
        processing. The winner will be judged on the advancement of 
        the science and the use of technology. 

     The contest will run from June through November, 1995. Entry 
applications will be available from PSC beginning June 1, and all 
proposals are to be submitted to PSC. 

     The recently announced AlphaServer 8400 system is the 
industry's most powerful server for demanding commercial and 
scientific computing, until now the domain of significantly more 
expensive mainframes and supercomputers.

     "The AlphaServer 8400 system is quite appropriate for this 
contest -- it stretches the limits of commercial and technical 
computing," added Nist. "This new system has made a notable 
impression across the industry, delivering the highest RISC 
performance and best price/performance at one-tenth the price of
the most widely used mainframes."

     The winner in each category will be announced and awarded in 
mid-November, 1995.  Winners will be honored at the SuperComputing 
'95 conference in December in San Diego. 

     More information and complete contest rules will be available 
over the Internet from Digital:

		http://www.digital.com/info/hpc/tl_contest.http
and PSC:
		http://pscinfo.psc.edu/

Certain restrictions will apply. Void where prohibited. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
