          DIGITAL PROVIDES SUN MICROSYSTEMS USERS PAINLESS
         MIGRATION PATH TO ADVANCED, 64-BIT ALPHA PLATFORMS


    ...FreePort Express translates Sun Applications in minutes...


SAN FRANCISCO, California -- May 23, 1995 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation today announced FreePort Express, a free migration tool 
which provides Sun Microsystems users the freedom to move off their 
legacy systems and directly onto today's performance leader, 
Digital's 64-bit Alpha systems family. The FreePort Express 
translator translates user executable code for SPARC - based systems 
running Solaris 1.x (formerly SunOS) directly into Alpha executable
code that runs on Digital UNIX(R) in minutes without having to port 
source code. 

     "Digital is the clear performance leader in the industry," said 
Philippe Ribeyre, vice president of Digital's Workstation Business 
Segment. "Many Sun users recognize that their systems have run out 
of gas and that they face at least two major transitions as they 
move to 64-bit architectures. FreePort Express gets them to 21st 
Century computing with no pain." 

     The FreePort Express translator builds on Digital's long 
experience in providing migration products to ease the transition of 
applications from one platform to another. Most of the applications 
available on Sun platforms run under Solaris 1.x, a version of UNIX 
based on the Berkeley kernel.  FreePort Express allows users to quickly 
and easily upgrade their computing environments to Digital UNIX and the 
Alpha architecture. 

     "Customers look for systems which provide for the growth they 
know is coming," said Ribeyre. "But they need to make a transition 
which capitalizes on the software investments they have in- house 
today. FreePort Express is the tool which preserves the software 
investment while opening the door for a superior computing 
environment using Alpha systems." 

     "Digital's binary translator, FreePort Express, delivers a 
previously missing piece of the integration puzzle by greatly 
facilitating application migration form SunOS to Digital UNIX," said 
Greg Weiss, research analyst with D. H. Brown Associates, Inc. "The 
new tools clearly justify Digital's claim to offer better migration 
from SunOS than Sun itself provides." 

     FreePort Express will be provided to Sun customers and software 
developers at no cost in July. 

